{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Repriced P1 → P4, and the description rewritten\n\nThis ticket was filed at P1 claiming a signing-correctness hole in which a `completion-verifier` signature could be minted over a stale tree. That claim was not supported by any observation. It was inferred from reading the source, and then written up with a severity and a store-wide census attached to substantiate it.\n\nThe investigation that produced the reading had already stated the hedge correctly — \"the mechanism for a signed false PASS is demonstrated; an actual instance is not\" — and the hedge was dropped when the ticket was written.\n\nWhat is actually on the record: one incident, on `8806-198c-e2d6-46ce`, which was a false FAIL. The verifier could not see work that had merged. That is the safe direction, and it is the opposite of the failure this ticket asserted.\n\nA further gap the original draft did not acknowledge: it was never established what the post-verify drift check is *for*. If its purpose is to detect the tree moving during verification, then comparing the pin against itself across that window is the intended behaviour and there is no defect at all. Settling that costs a reading of the check's intent, and it comes before anything else here.\n\nThe census is now out of scope. Inventing expensive work to substantiate an unobserved hypothesis is the thing that went wrong, not a remedy for it."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789785261996856478,"uuid":"126d4983-db21-4776-8ec6-cf32148303bf"}